{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778550150,"nonce":"RmwYchzyWkELWi+x7Xe10MNWsNc=","title":"Include error output in auth:resolved channel events","message":"When an auth request fails (exit_code \u003e 0), the `auth:resolved` SSE event and the corresponding channel-event payload carry `status`, `exit_code`, `outcome`, and `head_branch` — but NOT the stderr/stdout from the auth-merge execution. The agent receiving the channel event has to make a separate API call to diagnose the failure, which is an unnecessary round-trip.\n\n**Requested change:** Include an `error` or `output` field in the `auth:resolved` event payload containing the last N lines (e.g. 20) of the executor's stderr when `exit_code \u003e 0`. The channel-event formatter (`buildAuthChannelEvent` in `channel-server/notifications.ts`) already handles an `error` field — it just needs the daemon to populate it.\n\n**Where to change:**\n- `src/auth-queue.ts`: `buildResolvedPayload` should include the captured stderr when exit_code is non-zero\n- The executor capture mechanism (wherever auth-merge stdout/stderr is collected) needs to store the output in the auth_request row or pass it through the event\n\nSurfaced from a real failure: a dotfiles merge failed with 'Working tree is dirty' but the channel event only carried exit_code=1 with no explanation, requiring an extra API call + user report to diagnose.\n\nCross-ref: `4e32923` (channel-event delivery)","files":null}]}